A||||18||Mach10||Decklid Torsion rod||||5||
Z||000000||Mach10||05-26-2001||11:09 PM||||I do have one beef with my new toy. When you hit the trunk-release button, the decklid comes flying up with so much force that it will move the car, in neutral a few inches. I am worried that the damn thing will rip off the hinges with time. Is there any way to loosen things up, or will I have to get a pneumatic system in? <P>I will, however cherish the memory of my friend's Slurpee sailing over the hood of my car after he asked me to "pop the hood!"||||reg||5
Z||000001||Nashco||05-26-2001||11:20 PM||||Do you have a wing on it?  Perhaps you should <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||PBJ||05-26-2001||11:29 PM||||Thats what mine did when I had my wing off for repainting. optional catapolt or <BR>slushy-a-polt.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GTPete.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mach10||05-26-2001||11:45 PM||||You mean the weight of the wing is enough to slow it down!?!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><BR>*grabs GM engineer and slaps, hard.*<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||Formula88||05-27-2001||12:05 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>You mean the weight of the wing is enough to slow it down!?!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><BR>*grabs GM engineer and slaps, hard.*<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well, sure. It's only about 10lbs, but it is at the end of the decklid, giving it a pretty good mechanical advantage of force.  <P>Slushy loaded.....Fire 1!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Nashco||05-27-2001||12:13 AM||||Of course it's enough weight, like Formula88 said.  Just think about it; one slushy hardly slows it down, but think if you had 12 on there...then it would only tip the slushies over, not nearly enough to launch it over the car <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||sjp777||05-27-2001||12:51 AM||||If it's flying up that hard, the torsion rods are set up for a winged decklid. I don't remember if the torsion rods are actually different for the 2 versions, or merely mounted differently. Either way, something is not right. It shouldn't be going up that hard.<P>BTW, if you're going to play around with them, don't unbolt the torsion rods from the decklid mount bracket without tying them off to something first. They will launch themselves through the back window.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/890sm_2.jpg">    <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A><p>[This message has been edited by sjp777 (edited 05-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Steve Normington||05-27-2001||03:47 AM||||I thought I had already posted here, but I can't find it.<P>There are colour codes for the springs. White springs are the stronger, red/blue springs are weaker. I seem to remeber no colour springs that are the weakest. If your decklid is going to stay bare, they you might want to switch springs. I'm sure you can find someone that would want the white springs.<P>PS. The colours are a small faint patch of paint near the bottom of the spring on the opposite side of the hinge. IE, the colour spring for the left hinge is under the right side hinge.||||reg||1
Z||000008||skitime||05-27-2001||06:52 AM||||I think the torsion struts mounts have several different holes to adjust tension. Three if I remember.  Be very careful removing the tension on the struts or the arm will sail through your back window.  You will need a pipe or something to hold the pressure while releasing it.  After removing the tension you can adjust the strut into a different hole.  <BR>Be careful and safe.<P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL><BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pictures-1_5.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by skitime (edited 05-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||87GTZ34||05-27-2001||09:56 AM||||I think there are different positions for the the torsion bars as well. I will take a look later and post if no one has already done so.||||reg||1
Z||000010||fierohoho||05-27-2001||01:31 PM||||Mach10, I recently adjusted my springs for more tension due to adding a wing, now I don't know about spring colors but I did adjust my spring for max tension with little trouble.<P>I did it with the hood in place. I had to stand in the trunk. I used an 8" pipe wrench to pull back on the spring to relieve presure on the pin, (here it would be nice to have had help)I pulled the pin and keeping tension on the spring put it through the rear most of the three holes then released the tension on the wrench. I did this to both sides. <P>my trunk doesn't fly up but it will stay up now in a wind. Good luck.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||cadaver||05-27-2001||01:36 PM||||How do you get it to go through the back window? I have done many spring changes and never had a problem. <P>Just open the lid. Grab the spring to pull it back from the pin and pull out the pin. I dont know if you can adjust the heavy spring light enough to get it to be ok without a wing. But they swap ont in like 20 minutes.||||reg||1
Z||000012||terryk||05-27-2001||06:20 PM||||Oh yes. Follow the instructions in the manual to a tee or the rear window is history.<P>Hey, that rhymes!||||reg||1
Z||000013||Mach10||05-28-2001||01:22 AM||||Well, I looked, and I see white. Go figure. I'm actually considering just adding one of those hydraulic lifters to it. That'd slow it down, as well as ensure that it always opens when asked...<P>My mechanic's had a torsion rod go through his back window too. He wasn't pleased. I'm a little leary of doing this myself. Actually, I could probably wedge a chunk of 3/4" plywood between the window and the spring. That'd do it, hmm?||||reg||7
Z||000014||cadaver||05-28-2001||02:54 PM||||So instead of just doing it the right way you are going to add more stuff to it. Those gas shocks will probably make it go up faster as they ARE designed to support a load not slow one.<BR>Are the stiff springs heavy enough if you pull the pin on one side and just use one spring to hold the lid up. They are designed to support alot more weight than a lid with no wing.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Steve Normington||05-28-2001||04:38 PM||||cadaver is right, either remove the white springs and add the strut or change the white springs for red/blue. I do have to disagree with the suggestion to only use one spring. I think that would be too little force and it would put a lot of side load on the hinges. It's not that tough to find a pair of blue/red springes, and the change over is pretty simple if you have help.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Mach10||05-28-2001||07:26 PM||||DOUBLE POST<p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 05-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||Mach10||05-28-2001||07:27 PM||||Sorry, that was a wording problem. Yes, I meant replacing the torsion rod system with a hydraulic system. I like the action much better on a hydro system, anyway. Nicer, smoother, and above all, CONTROLLED  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000018||cadaver||05-28-2001||11:16 PM||||CONTROLLED? with the proper springs installed and adjusted properly the lid doesnt come up at all. <BR>When I push the trunk release all it does is just unlock the trunk and it lift like an inch. Then you lift it yourself. I have even drove down the street with it popped until i say the AJAR light. <BR>But to each his own. ||||reg||1
